00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.190 align:start position:0%
snap true reality introduces the  automatic alignment of virtual objects  to physical constraints by calculating  those constraints from the real world in  real time  positioning virtual content and space  around a user is an important task in  many augmented reality applications  while it is possible to do so without  regard to the real world surrounding the  user many augmented reality experiences  tend to require a tight alignment  between the virtual and real environment  controlling the six degrees of freedom  to position and orient content so that  it is precisely aligned with the real  world can be quite a challenging task  that might otherwise be very easy with  snapping in this work we present a  proof-of-concept system that allows one  to use the physical geometry around them  to easily position virtual objects in  their environment our prototype uses a  Microsoft Kinect sensor Lumis optical  see-through glasses and an optic track  system to maintain the relative pose  between the user and the Kinect our  system specifically focuses on  extracting physical edges and planar  surfaces as we believe they represent a  rich set of constraints common to many  environments edges and surfaces are  extracted by analyzing the color depth  and surface normals of the environment  as the environment dynamically evolves  the definition of the constraints may  change to represent the new geometry we  take special care to minimize any  temporal noise and allow using  constraints that change smoothly over  time we can detect a variety of physical  constraints in many different  environments that can be used for  guiding the position and modification of  virtual objects please see our paper for  a detailed description of our algorithm  to further investigate snapping an  augmented reality we conducted to user  experiments in the first experiment  participants position and skilled  augmented reality app windows to specify  targets with and without snapping if the  app window is not close enough to the  target we notify the user to try again  participants positioned virtual contents  significantly faster with snapping than  without participants also indicated that  they felt more fatigued while not using  snapping than with using snapping this  is indicated by observing the difference  in physical movement by the participants  under both conditions in the second  experiment participants created virtual  3d objects guided by constraints  extracted from the physical environment  a 3d box lying on the surface parallel  to edges and walls and the desired  dimensions could be constructed in  seconds easy in Scituate enabled  participants to be quite creative such  as creating virtual shelves extruding  cubes to Heights of dynamic scene  elements or using a physical measuring  tape to guide high extrusions in summary  snap to reality online meant techniques  help users align virtual content to  real-world constraints please see our  paper for more details including an  analysis of design considerations for  future snap to reality systems  you
 

